<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aloysius Gonzaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1568">1568</a>–<a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Aloysius, Luigi Gonzaga · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">House of Gonzaga</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aloysius Gonzaga was an Italian noble and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> saint. The eldest son of the Marquis of Castiglione, he gave up his rights of succession to his brother to join the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1585. He died aged 23 after nursing victims of an epidemic in Rome. Canonised in 1726, he became the patron saint of young people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aloysius-gonzaga.jpg" alt="Aloysius Gonzaga" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guercino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Vocation_of_Saint_Aloysius_Gonzaga.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aloysius, in Italian Luigi, was born on 9 March 1568 at his family's castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castiglione-delle-stiviere">Castiglione delle Stiviere</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and Mantua, the eldest of eight children of Ferrante Gonzaga, Marquess of Castiglione, and Marta Tana di Santena. His father expected him to become a soldier and sent him to a military camp at five. In 1576 he and his brother Rodolfo were sent to the court of the Grand Duke Francesco I de' Medici at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, where a kidney disease began that troubled him all his life. He read about the saints and prayed much.</p><p>He received his First Communion from Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Charles Borromeo</a> on 22 July 1580. After reading a book about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> he wanted to become a missionary. In 1582 the family were at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, where he and Rodolfo served as pages to the Infante Diego, and he decided to join the Jesuits. His father was furious and prevented him, and after the family's return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1584 relatives tried to persuade him to become a secular priest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In November 1585 Gonzaga gave up all rights of inheritance, which was confirmed by the emperor, and on 25 November 1585 he was accepted into the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He was asked to moderate his asceticism. On 25 November 1587 he took the three vows of chastity, poverty and obedience, and in 1588 he began to study theology.</p><p>In 1591 a plague broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> opened a hospital, and Gonzaga volunteered, begging alms and carrying the dying from the streets. He confessed privately to his spiritual director, Robert Bellarmine, that he had to work hard to overcome his physical repulsion. He was infected and was bedridden by 3 March 1591, a few days before his 23rd birthday.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died just before midnight on 21 June 1591, after Bellarmine gave him the last rites. He was buried in the church that became Sant'Ignazio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Pope Paul V beatified him on 19 October 1605, and Pope Benedict XIII canonised him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> novice Stanislaus Kostka on 31 December 1726. In 1729 Benedict XIII declared him patron of youth and students. His feast day is 21 June. Schools and colleges bear his name, and in art he is shown with a lily, a cross, a skull and a rosary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aloysius Gonzaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ferrante's eldest son <b>Aloysius</b>, born in 1568, renounced his rights to join the Jesuits and died in 1591 nursing plague victims in Rome; he was canonised in 1726 and the town became a pilgrimage centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-castiglione">Principality of Castiglione</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castiglione-delle-stiviere">Castiglione delle Stiviere</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.4,10.5&amp;year=1582" aria-label="Castiglione delle Stiviere on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aloysius Gonzaga"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5627 6185h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5627 6185h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.4,10.5&amp;year=1582">What was here in 1582? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
